the secret language that we all speak  means every bit of nonverbal  communication that we use to interact  with other people in our relationships  and the interesting thing is that we are  all experts in it so everybody that  walks into every lecture that I give is  already an expert just navigating the  line at coffee and traffic and walking  on public transit you're speaking that  language in negotiating who moves out of  the way  what gestures do you do and who takes  what seat is all communicating in this  language of power an example cocktail  party there's a circle of people you're  standing around talking and someone  starts to approach that circle of people  is going to either open up and invite  that person in or it's going to close  ranks a little because it's not for  whatever reason okay for that person to  enter the conversation needs to go a  particular way because of who that  person is whatever the context that  happens all the time it happens in bars  and at coffee houses everywhere we go so  that's also communicating in this  language of power and it's something we  don't think about the group doesn't have  a vote on how we're going to behave they  just react then it's six or seven people  reacting to small verbal cues small  nonverbal cues all simultaneously  any one individual can change the  dynamic of the group or the person  they're with and they do it by a set of  behaviors what we're talking about is  the difference between being more  authoritative or being more approachable  so if you want to joy the circle you can  enter the circle before you even  approach it by calling out someone's  name and that puts you inside that  circle already you could come up and tap  someone on the shoulder or gently nudge  someone and all of that nonverbal  communication is asserting I'm here I'm  taking an authoritative approach that  I'm going to enter it's a little on the  dominant side of things  conversely if you're in the circle you  can decide I'm going to let that person  in and you can use your power from  within the circle and take up the space  and say I own this and because I do come  and join me come into my space I'm  holding in the circle here and I'm going  to let you in and share my power with  you  I typically show up with a little bit  more motion my head's a little to the  side and I tend to be friendly in fact  people come up on the street and ask me  questions all over the world when I'm  not local directions or other things  because I'm that way but I can change by  just making myself a little more still a  little more upright I can make my voice  go a little bit lower into my chest  voice I can speak a little bit more  slowly and I can leave more space in my  words slow down my gestures I would say  across that last 30 seconds I became a  lot more grounded and authoritative and  like you would believe the directions I  was giving you in London even if I  didn't know them  conversely if that's how you always show  up if you tend to be in this slow place  you can make small changes you can talk  just a little bit faster maybe you can  tilt your head a little bit you can nod  maybe you know touch your chin a little  bit as you talk or a little bit more  gesture with your hand and I think this  is a little friendlier it's a little  softer so just by making those small  changes two things happen one I'm  communicating to you a different set of  social cues about that you can approach  me or that you shouldn't  and as it turns out by changing your  body you change your internal state in  one place where I'm more authoritative I  will begin to feel more authoritative  just by taking on those gestures and I  will feel a little more friendly and  approachable by taking on the other set  of gestures so it works both ways you  can decide to be it inside and it will  come out naturally and you can decide to  put it on physically and it will go  inside  after you start thinking about this you  can't avoid it you'll see it as you walk  across campus or as you go to get a  coffee but it's just the background  that's happening because it's naturally  how we communicate you don't have to  make strategic choices all day long that  would be exhausting but most of us have  learned this navigation as children and  as we come up and we sort a lot of it  out in junior high school it's all these  social cues and it's just how we  function in the world so we're on  autopilot most of the time and that  works great for 90% of the people it  works great there are a few but it works  great all the time when it's really  important when it makes a difference you  might want to consider some different  choices when it's an important meeting  for you when it isn't going well when  you're having a challenging situation  these are the points where you want when  it might want to stop and take a thought  and a little bit of action and then you  can go back to just being you because  most of us are successful well adjusted  people that navigate this without  thinking and that's how it should be  often when we come home from a stressful  day and we walk in the house we carry  all of that along with us and it just  comes along because that commute was  rough and that guy cut us off and and  all of that's inside and as I said  what's inside comes out and so we walk  in the house but you can stop right at  the door and take 30 seconds or a minute  and reset think about what's on the  other side of the door how much you care  about them how you want to show up for  them what fun you have had or planning  to have and that will start to change  you you can put a smile on and change  your body and then when you walk through  the door you're a different you  everybody's fear primarily is that the  way they are isn't the way they should  be that what if I was the other side of  the equation and then most people are  afraid of actually being powerful they  say they're not our students come in and  they 